[25.450335, "o", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'There'\"'\"'s something wrong with my system-wide python installation - I can'\"'\"'t seem to install packages with pip.'; tmux wait -S done\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[106.830935, "o", "\r\n```\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in .../site-packages (24.3.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n`ensurepip` consults installed metadata to decide whether to act. The orphaned `dist-info` asserted pip 24.3.1 was installed, so the installer skipped the work — leaving you exactly as broken as before, with a success exit code. Worth remembering: `ensurepip` reporting success is not evidence that pip exists.\r\n\r\n## The fix\r\n\r\n1. Moved the lying metadata aside: `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` → `/tmp/pip-orphan-backup/`\r\n2. `/usr/local/bin/python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip` — this time it installed from the wheel bundled inside CPython itself, so **no network access was needed** to repair pip.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\nI wrote the checks as a script that fails non-zero, at `.gnomon-out/verify_pip.sh` (that path is gitignored, so it won't be committed):\r\n\r\n```\r\nPASS  python3 imports pip\r\nPASS  pip3 reports a version\r\nPASS  pip (bare name) works\r\nPASS  python -m pip works\r\nPASS  dependency graph intact\r\nPASS  pip package dir is r"]
[106.831035, "o", "eal\r\nPASS  exactly one pip dist-info\r\nPASS  can resolve deps from PyPI\r\n\r\nALL CHECKS PASSED   (exit 0)\r\n```\r\n\r\nBeyond the script, a real end-to-end install into a throwaway venv: `pip install packaging` → `import packaging` → `26.3`. `pip check` reports `No broken requirements found.` I used venvs for these tests deliberately — I did not install third-party packages into the system site-packages just to prove it works. `--dry-run` confirmed the system pip can also resolve a full dependency tree (`requests` → certifi, charset-normalizer, idna, urllib3).\r\n\r\n## Two things to know\r\n\r\n**A second, separate Python exists and still has no pip.** `/usr/bin/python3` is Python **3.11.2**, distinct from the 3.13.1 you were hitting. It has no pip either, but I left it alone on purpose: it carries `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED`, so it's a distro-managed interpreter where system-wide `pip install` is blocked by design. Using `pip` against it is the wrong move — use `python3 -m venv` there. Your 3.13 has "]
[106.831073, "o", "no such marker, so it accepts installs.\r\n\r\n**Your pip is 24.3.1 and 26.2.2 is available.** I did not upgrade it, since that wasn't the problem and upgrading a system pip is a change you may want to make deliberately. When you do, prefer `python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip` in a user or venv context.\r\n\r\nRoot cause is worth a second look on your side: pip's code was removed but its metadata wasn't, so whatever deleted it ran non-atomically. If that was an image build or a provisioning script, it will likely do the same thing again on the next run.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=29 surface=a825037b110a\r\n"]
